,51,456],294.0048,0.50732,-1.440795,[458,459,51,460],297.2715,0.544271,-1.426817,[462,463,51,464],300.5382,0.580774,-1.411661,[466,467,51,468],303.8049,0.616725,-1.395164,[89,470,51,471],0.662196,-1.372237,[473,474,51,475],310.3384,0.668402,-1.369226,[477,474,51,475],313.6051,[479,474,51,475],316.8718,[481,474,51,475],320.1385,[483,474,51,475],323.4053,"MAVEN","ORBITER · FLOWN","First mission dedicated to studying how Mars lost its atmosphere","MAVEN — Mars Atmosphere and Volatile Evolution — answers the question 'where did Mars's air go?' Its elliptical orbit dips into the upper atmosphere, sampling ions and neutrals as the solar wind strips them away. Findings: Mars loses about 100 g of atmosphere per second to space — over billions of years that's enough to explain the missing climate. The spacecraft also served as a critical relay for surface assets, including Curiosity and Perseverance, until it lost contact in December 2025; NASA declared the mission over in June 2026.",[489,493,495,498],{"met":51,"label":490,"note":491,"type":492},"LAUNCH","Atlas V 401 from Cape Canaveral SLC-41.","nominal",{"met":61,"label":63,"note":494,"type":492},"First trajectory correction.",{"met":12,"label":496,"note":497,"type":492},"MARS ORBIT INSERTION","33-minute main engine burn captures MAVEN into a 35-hour elliptical science orbit.",{"met":499,"label":500,"note":501,"type":502},350,"FIRST SCIENCE","Begins atmosphere-sampling 'deep dip' campaigns to periapsis altitudes ~125 km.","info","/orrery/images/missions/maven/01.webp"],"uses":{"params":["id"]}}]}
